INT. BEEKEEPER'S KITCHEN - MORNING

Honey-gold light slants through a window crusted with propolis. On the table, a cracked jar leaks slow amber across oilcloth. FLORIAN ACHTERBERG (70s, veil pushed back, hands like roots) stands rigid at the stove. His daughter MAREN (40s, city coat, car keys still in her fist) has not sat down. At the table, her son EZRA (9, scabbed elbows) hides behind a comic book, while his aunt ODALYS (50s, flour on her apron) pretends to knead dough.

MAREN
You sold the north hives without telling me.

FLORIAN
They were mine to sell.

ODALYS
Maren, sit. There's tea.

MAREN
I don't want tea. Dad, those were Mom's.

FLORIAN
Your mother is dead eleven years. The bees don't know it.

EZRA
(from behind the comic)
Are we fighting?

ODALYS
No, sweetheart. We're discussing.

MAREN
I drove six hours. I thought you were sick.

FLORIAN
I am old. It's the same thing.

MAREN
(breaking)
You could have asked me.

FLORIAN
And you would have said keep them. And I would have kept them. And they would have died, because I can't lift the supers anymore.

Silence. A single bee drifts across the room and lands on the leaking jar.

ODALYS
Ezra, bring me the dish towel.

He does. Maren sits at last. Florian turns from the stove, picks up the jar, and sets it in front of her.

FLORIAN
Taste.

MAREN
Dad.

FLORIAN
Taste it.

She dips a finger. Her face softens as she tastes, and she covers her mouth with her wrist.

MAREN
It's hers.

FLORIAN
The last of the north. I saved it for you.
